many people think of Sciences a  political concerned with facts not  political views values or opinions  however when it comes to science-based  policy and legislation science no longer  seems unbiased the prevalence of  opinions and biases and policy debates  has led countless members of the  scientific community to avoid policy  debates despite their ability to provide  helpful contributions  however science and politics are more  intertwined than most people realize  science helps lawmakers write policies  and regulations that affect our everyday  lives at the same time government shape  science through budgets laws and  regulations using NASA the National  Aeronautics and Space Administration as  an example let's walk through how our  federal policies have shaped research in  2018 Congress provided NASA with an  annual operating budget of sixteen point  five billion dollars why did the federal  government invest so heavily in NASA's  research first public demand for  scientific research can strongly  influence politicians motivated by  increasing public concern lawmakers have  funded research to investigate climate  changes causes and mitigate its effects  this research is closely tied to NASA's  work in outer space satellites monitor  Earth's atmosphere and solar panels  which power most spacecraft are an  emerging alternative to fossil fuels  Congress also funds research because it  recognizes the potential for research to  have broad societal impacts in  unexpected ways as a result many  groundbreaking technologies like GPS and  advanced prosthetic limbs have their  roots in NASA technology second global  competition and national Security's  concerns motivate federal funding for  NASA the space race which was one of the  many arenas in which the United States  and the Soviet Union fought the Cold War  assured in NASA's growth  after the Soviet Union shocked the  United States by building Sputnik the  world's first orbiting spacecraft NASA's  successful moon landing in 1969 restored  national pride and garnered  international recognition NASA has also  developed numerous military aviation  technologies like composite materials  fly-by-wire cockpits and wing tip  winglets that were later adapted for  commercial aircraft government  priorities shift with time and this is  reflected in NASA's budget for example  near the height of the space race in  1966 the fraction of the federal budget  dedicated to NASA was almost ten times  as large as it is today by contrast  adjusted for today's dollars the  National Institute of Health's budget  grew threefold between 1970 in 2005 now  let's examine how science influences  policy many beneficial laws like the  Clean Air Act of 1970 have been the  result of scientific research and  scientists are frequently consulted as  experts when bills are drafted  scientific research also serves as the  basis for entire federal institutions  like the Department of Energy which  regulates nuclear material and funds  energy related research as we can see  scientific research shapes policy and  vice-versa every generation has new  challenges that need scientific  solutions ensuring that our nation's  students are provided the education and  training to meet these challenges both  through science and policy is crucial to  the success of our nation and the world  you
 

